INT. FUNICULAR CAR, MIDWAY UP - NIGHT

A glass cabin on a steel cable, hanging over a sleeping city. Lights below like spilled salt. The cable hums.

Six people. A BRIEFCASE MAN, fifties, tie loosened. A WOMAN IN RED, thirties, perched on the bench. A TEENAGER, hood up, earbuds in. A PRIEST, seventies. A MECHANIC, big hands. A CONDUCTOR, grey cap, bored.

The car shudders. Stops. Lights flicker.

CONDUCTOR
Folks, a brief delay. Remain seated.

BRIEFCASE MAN
How brief?

CONDUCTOR
A few minutes.

WOMAN IN RED
Then why is he sweating?

All eyes turn to the briefcase man. He grips the case to his chest.

BRIEFCASE MAN
I'm not.

TEENAGER
(pulling out an earbud)
He is.

PRIEST
My son, are you unwell?

BRIEFCASE MAN
I'm fine!

MECHANIC
That case is ticking.

Silence. The ticking is audible now, quick and mean.

BRIEFCASE MAN
It's a watch. A prototype.

WOMAN IN RED
Open it.

CONDUCTOR
Sir, I'll have to ask you to open it.

He does not. The Woman in Red lunges. The Mechanic grabs her arm. The Teenager is shouting. The Priest has his hand on the briefcase man's shoulder, murmuring.

MECHANIC
Hold him!

BRIEFCASE MAN
(breaking)
My name is Aldous Prewitt, I'm a courier, I was told it was documents!

PREWITT (CONT'D)
I swear it, I swear it on my mother!

The case clicks. The lid lifts. Inside, an old brass alarm clock rings, sits on a stack of unmarked bills, and a note reads: GOT YOU ALL TO TALK.

The funicular lurches. Lights return. The car climbs on.
